ping this afternoon will be capable\nof producing rainfall rates in excess of 3\"/hr. Flash flooding is\npossible this afternoon, especially in more urbanized locations.\n\nDISCUSSION...GOES East satellite shows mostly sunny skies over\nsouth FL and widespread cloud cover over the northern half of the\nFL Peninsula. This is generally a good demarcation for the\nlocation of a stalled frontal boundary located over central FL\nthat will become the focus for thunderstorms this afternoon. The\ncontrast between cloud cover to the north and clearer skies to the\nsouth will tighten the thermal/CAPE gradient already in place. The\nenvironment is primed to produce highly efficient rainfall rates.\nThe 12Z TBW (Tampa) sounding measured a 2.46\" PW and MLCAPE >800\nJ/kg. Low-to-mid level RH values were above 90%, and RH values\n>80% were common within the 500-150mb layer, highlighting the\nimpressive moisture content available within the troposphere.\n\nAmong the other notable parameters is the vertical wind shear\npresent. The 12Z TBW sounding measured cloud layer shear levels\nnear 30 kts. Per SPC sounding climatology, this is around the 90th\nclimatological percentile. In addition, much of the state is\nlocated beneath the right-entrance region of a 250mb jet streak\nwhich is providing excellent upper-level divergence aloft. Between\nthe anomalous moisture content, the frontal boundary acting as a\nsurface-based trigger, and the synoptic-scale forcing aloft,\n### wpc/MPD1461_20261008T2046Z.txt\n276 \nAWUS01 KWNH 082046\nFFGMPD\nFLZ000-090200-\n\nMesoscale Precipitation Discussion 1461\nNWS Weather Prediction Center College Park MD\n446 PM EDT Thu Oct 08 2026\n\nAreas affected...Florida Peninsula\n\nConcerning...Heavy rainfall...Flash flooding possible\n\nValid 082045Z - 090200Z\n\nSUMMARY...Scattered thunderstorms capable of producing >3\"/hr\nrainfall rates may produce flash flooding in poor drainage areas\nand in highly urbanized communities.\n\nDISCUSSION...20Z Doppler Radar showed scattered thunderstorms over\ncentral and southern FL, while some occasional elevated convection\nflared up over northern FL. The environment remains highly\nsaturated with PWs reaching 2.5\" in some instances. The nearby\nfrontal boundary has played a role in triggering thunderstorms in\ncentral FL, while the western and eastern coasts of FL have seen\nstorms flare up along sea breeze fronts. From Tampa on east to\nCape Canaveral and points south, RAP mesoanalysis shows >1,000\nJ/kg of MLCAPE available to developing storms. The 300mb jet\ncirculation pattern also shows upper-level divergence remains\npresent within the right-entrance region of a 300mb jet streak.\n\nLatest RAP forecast shows unusually high levels of vertical wind\nshear lingering into the evening hours, as will the sufficient\ninstability and copious amounts of moisture aloft. It will not\ntake much from any lifting mechanism; sea breezes, outflow\nboundaries, or the frontal boundary to generate additional storms\nthrough 00Z. Most CAMs are in agreement that the coverage of\nthunderstorms should begin to dwindle not long after 00Z and most\nstorms should dissipate by midnight EDT. The available moisture\nand instability, along with the potential for some training\n### wpc/PMDSPD_20261007T0811Z.txt\n209 \nFXUS01 KWBC 070811\nPMDSPD\n\nShort Range Forecast Discussion\nNWS Weather Prediction Center College Park MD\n359 AM EDT Wed Oct 07 2026\n\nValid 12Z Wed Oct 07 2026 - 12Z Fri Oct 09 2026\n\n...The ongoing October heatwave across central and southern\nCalifornia begins to show signs of letting up by Friday...\n\n...Watching the future progress of tropical cyclone Nine in the\nGulf as lingering heavy rainfall and isolated flood threats in\nFlorida begin to move north later on Thursday...\n\nAn increasingly active weather pattern is in store heading into\nthe latter half of the week as tropical activity ramps up and gets\ncaught amid major changes in the upper-air pattern in the\nmid-latitudes. First of all, the October heatwave continues today\nacross portions of California and the Desert Southwest, where\ntemperatures remain 15-20 degrees above average with highs\nreaching into the 90s to low 100s, with overnight lows in the mid-\nto upper 70s providing little relief from the heat. Major to\nlocally Extreme HeatRisk means that the heat is dangerous to\nanyone without adequate cooling or hydration where Heat Advisories\nand Extreme Heat Warnings remain in effect. Temperatures are\nexpected to gradually cool for coastal California Thursday and\nthen interior areas by Friday so some relief from the heat is in\nsight. While not dangerously hot, conditions across the western to\ncentral U.S. remain well above average for October with widespread\nhighs in the 70s and 80s. Some daily record highs will be\nchallenged or broken. On the other hand, conditions remain cooler\nand below average along the East Coast and particularly for the\nNortheast. Frost and Freeze related Advisories and Warnings are in\nplace for portions of New England into northeastern Pennsylvania\nearly this morning. Temperatures will moderate quite a bit over\nthe next couple of days with much warmer highs into the 70s\nexpected for most locations by Thursday, while a brief intrusion\n### wpc/PMDSPD_20261007T0909Z.txt\n019 \nFXUS01 KWBC 070909\nPMDSPD\n\nShort Range Forecast Discussion\nNWS Weather Prediction Center College Park MD\n506 AM EDT Wed Oct 07 2026\n\nValid 12Z Wed Oct 07 2026 - 12Z Fri Oct 09 2026\n\n...The ongoing October heatwave across central and southern\nCalifornia begins to show signs of letting up by Friday...\n\n...Watching the future progress of Tropical Storm Isaias in the\nGulf as lingering heavy rainfall and isolated flood threats in\nFlorida begin to move north later on Thursday...\n\nAn increasingly active weather pattern is in store heading into\nthe latter half of the week as tropical activity ramps up and gets\ncaught amid major changes in the upper-air pa