g into the 90s to\nlow 100s and overnight lows in the mid to upper 70s providing\nlittle relief. Major to locally extreme HeatRisk means the heat is\ndangerous to anyone without adequate cooling or hydration. Heat\nAdvisories and Extreme Heat Warnings remain in effect until\ntemperatures gradually cool for coastal California on Thursday and\ninterior areas by Friday. While not dangerously hot, conditions\nacross the western to central United States remain well above\naverage for October, with widespread highs in the 70s and 80s\nchallenging or breaking some daily record highs. On the other\nhand, conditions remain cooler and below average along the East\nCoast and particularly for the Northeast, though temperatures will\nmoderate quite a bit over the next couple of days with much warmer\nhighs into the 70s expected for most locations by Thursday,\nfollowed by a brief intrusion of cool air reaching interior New\nEngland by Friday.\n\nAcross the Florida Peninsula, widespread thunderstorms will\npersist over the next couple of days as tropical moisture\ninteracts with a stationary boundary. Concurrently, Tropical Storm\nIsaias is projected to strengthen over the southwestern Gulf and\n### wpc/PMDSPD_20261008T0838Z.txt\n103 \nFXUS01 KWBC 080838\nPMDSPD\n\nShort Range Forecast Discussion\nNWS Weather Prediction Center College Park MD\n400 AM EDT Thu Oct 08 2026\n\nValid 12Z Thu Oct 08 2026 - 12Z Sat Oct 10 2026\n\n...Hurricane Isaias is forecast to intensify rapidly, bringing\ndamaging winds and torrential rain to portions of the Florida\nPanhandle, southern Alabama and southern Mississippi, with storm\nsurge for coastal communities by late on Friday...\n\n...October heatwave across central and southern California shows\nsign of relief by Friday...\n\n...High-elevation wet snow could reach the Canadian border of\nMontana and Washington by early Saturday morning...\n\nAn increasingly active weather pattern is in store heading into\nthe weekend as tropical activity ramps up and gets caught amid\nmajor changes in the upper-air pattern in the mid-latitudes. First\nof all, tropical cyclone Isaias has intensified into the first\nhurricane of the 2026 Atlantic hurricane season over the warm Gulf\nwater northwest of the Yucatan Peninsula early this morning.\nIsaias is forecast to intensify further as it gradually turns\nnorthward and makes landfall Friday evening when damaging\nhurricane-force winds and torrential rain are expected to impact\nareas from western Florida Panhandle to southern Alabama and\nsouthern Mississippi with storm surge for the coastal communities.\nAs Isaias turns northward over the next couple of days, the\ncirculation of the hurricane will continue to interact with a\nfrontal boundary extending east through Florida. This interaction\nwill gradually shift the heaviest rainfall associated with Isaias\ntoward the east side of the hurricane circulation over time. The\nWeather Prediction Center is highlighting a moderate risk of\nexcessive rainfall from across southern Alabama to the western\nportion of the Florida Peninsula from Friday to Saturday morning,\n### wpc/PMDSPD_20261008T1959Z.txt\n362 \nFXUS01 KWBC 081959\nPMDSPD\n\nShort Range Forecast Discussion\nNWS Weather Prediction Center College Park MD\n358 PM EDT Thu Oct 08 2026\n\nValid 00Z Fri Oct 09 2026 - 00Z Sun Oct 11 2026\n\n...Rapidly strengthening Hurricane Isaias threatens the northern\nGulf Coast (Mississippi to Florida Panhandle) with destructive\nwinds, severe storm surge, and torrential rain by late Friday...\n\n...Substantial cooling is on the way as a major pattern shift\nfinally breaks the prolonged October heatwave across central and\nsouthern California by Friday...\n\n...High-elevation wet snow could reach the Canadian border of\nMontana and Washington by early Saturday morning...\n\nAn active weather pattern is set to unfold this weekend as\ntropical development continues to progress. Hurricane Isaias, the\n2026 Atlantic season's inaugural storm, strengthened over the warm\nGulf waters northwest of the Yucatn Peninsula and is projected\nto intensify further ahead of a Friday evening landfall. The\nsystem threatens the region from the western Florida Panhandle to\nsouthern and central Alabama and Mississippi, and southwestern\nGeorgia with destructive hurricane-force winds, severe storm\nsurge, and torrential downpours. Hurricane warnings and tropical\nstorm warnings are currently in effect from as far west as extreme\neastern Louisiana to as far east as the Big Bend region of the\nFlorida Panhandle. A storm surge warning is also in effect for\nthese areas, and a tropical storm watch has expanded far inland\nacross parts of Mississippi, Alabama and Georgia. Flood Watches\nare now in effect for northern portions of Mississippi, Alabama,\nGeorgia, and much of western and central Tennessee.\n\nAs Hurricane Isaias approaches landfall, interaction with a\nstationary frontal boundary across Florida and an upper level\n### wpc/QPFERD_20261007T0012Z.txt\n018 \nFOUS30 KWBC 070012\nQPFERD\n\nExcessive Rainfall Discussion\nNWS Weather Prediction Center College Park MD\n812 PM EDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nDay 1\nValid 01Z Wed Oct 07 2026 - 12Z Wed Oct 07 2026\n\n...THERE IS A MARGINAL RISK OF EXCESSIVE RAINFALL FOR MUCH OF\nFLORIDA AND FAR SOUTHEASTERN GEORGIA...\n\n01Z Update...\nShort term trends in radar and satellite imagery support the\nHREF/REFS solutions for continuation of rainfall throughout the\nevening with higher probabilities for heavier rainfall amounts to\nbe focused along the coast. Urbanized and poor drainage areas\nremain more likely than other areas for instances of flash\nflooding. No changes needed to the areal outline of the Marginal\nRisk area.\n\nBann\n\n16Z Update...\n\nNo significant changes were made to the previous forecast. The\nHREF/REFS continue to indicate widespread amounts 1-3 inches are\nlikely within the Marginal Risk area, with higher probabilities for\nheavier totals focused along the coastal regions. This includes\nthe First Coast, where northeasterly flow on the north side of the\nstalled front is expected to support localized heavier amounts.\nUrbanized and poor drainage areas in and around the Jacksonville\nmetro are more likely to see instances of flash flooding.\n\nPereira\n\nPrevious discussion...\n\n### wpc/QPFERD_20261007T0828Z.txt\n833 \nFOUS30 KWBC 070828\nQPFERD\n\nExcessive Rainfall Discussion\nNWS Weather Prediction Center College Park MD\n428 AM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nDay 1\nValid 12Z Wed Oct 07 2026 - 12Z Thu Oct 08 2026\n\n...THERE IS A MARGINAL RISK OF EXCESSIVE RAINFALL FOR MUCH OF THE\nFLORIDA PENINSULA...\n\n...Florida...\n\nA very slow moving, but strong front will remain draped across the\nFlorida Peninsula today, specifically over the northern Florida\nPeninsula. A tropical air mass to the south of the front, over the\nPeninsula, characterized by PWATs above 2.25 inches will be\nuplifted against a much cooler and drier air mass to the north.\nThis clash of air masses will promote a continued rinse- and-\nrepeat pattern, with widespread light to moderate rainfall\ncontinuing along I-4, and generally south of I-10. As on previous\ndays, localized convection will remain possible. Where convection\nforms, the abundance of moisture will support locally heavy\nrainfall. The sluggish movement of any convection will prolong any\nassociated heavy rainfall. Thus, a Marginal Risk remains in place\nfor the possibility that any convection that develops will impact\nan urban or specifically flood-prone area.\n\n...Four Corners Region...\n\nWidely scattered showers and a few thunderstorms are possible\nacross the Four Corners Region this afternoon. Instability values\nare expected to peak on either side of 500 J/kg, which could allow\nfor any storms that form to produce locally heavy rainfall.\nConvective coverage appears to remain isolated, so the subsequent\nflash flooding potential appears to be sub-Marginal, but the area\nwill need to be monitored.\n### wpc/QPFERD_20261007T0903Z.txt\n718 \nFOUS30 KWBC 070903\nQPFERD\n\nExcessive Rainfall Discussion\nNWS Weather Prediction Center College Park MD\n503 AM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nDay 1\nValid 12Z Wed Oct 07 2026 - 12Z Thu Oct 08 2026\n\n...THERE IS A MARGINAL RISK OF EXCESSIVE RAINFALL FOR MUCH OF THE\nFLORIDA PENINSULA...\n\n...Florida...\n\nA very slow moving, but strong front will remain draped across the\nFlorida Peninsula today, specifically over the northern Florida\nPeninsula. A tropical air mass to the south of the front, over the\nPeninsula, characterized by PWATs above 2.25 inches will be\nuplifted against a much cooler and drier air mass to the north.\nThis clash of air masses will promote a continued rinse- and-\nrepeat pattern, with widespread light to moderate rainfall\ncontinuing along I-4, and generally south of I-10. As on previous\ndays, localized convection will remain possible. Where convection\nforms, the abundance of moisture will support locally heavy\nrainfall. The sluggish movement of any convection will prolong any\nassociated heavy rainfall. Thus, a Marginal Risk remains in place\nfor the possibility that any convection that develops will impact\nan urban or specifically flood-prone area.\n\n...Four Corners Region...\n\nWidely scattered showers and a few thunderstorms are possible\nacross the Four Corners Region this afternoon. Instability values\nare expected to peak on either side of 500 J/kg, which could allow\nfor any storms that form to produce locally heavy rainfall.\nConvective coverage appears to remain isolated, so the subsequent\nflash flooding potential appears to be sub-Marginal, but the area\nwill need to be monitored.\n### wpc/QPFERD_20261007T1550Z.txt\n593 \nFOUS30 KWBC 071550\nQPFERD\n\nExcessive Rainfall Discussion\nNWS Weather Prediction Center College Park MD\n1150 AM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nDay 1\nValid 16Z Wed Oct 07 2026 - 12Z Thu Oct 08 2026\n\n...THERE IS A MARGINAL RISK OF EXCESSIVE RAINFALL FOR THE LOWER\