morrow, before accelerating to the northeast and north\nThursday night through landfall on the northern Gulf Coast Friday\nnight as it phases with a southern stream shortwave trough.\nModels are slowly coming into better agreement, but there are\nstill some typical differences in track and timing. In general\nthe AI models remain further east than some of the physical\nmodels. Some models are also still a few hours apart on timing as\nwell. It's important to remember that the center of the storm can\ntrack anywhere inside (or even just outside) of the\n--- rainfall amounts char 58520 ---\nutions\nverify. Watches may become necessary as early as tonight.\n\nHeavy Rainfall/Flash Flooding:\n\nDrought remains ongoing over the area, but some areas did receive\nbeneficial rainfall last weekend, especially our far southwest\nand far southeast counties. Streamflows remain below normal to\nwell below normal for this time of year across West Alabama,\nwhile streamflows are closer to normal for this time of year\n(though normal is fairly low this time of year) elsewhere. This\nshould minimize the risk of widespread main stem river flooding.\nStill, with copious tropical moisture transport there will be a\nthreat for flash flooding, especially in urban and other poor\ndrainage areas. Current forecast rainfall amounts range from 2-4\"\nwith locally higher amounts of 5\". There may be a band of\nrelatively lower rainfall amounts due to dry slotting somewhere\nbetween higher amounts west with the core of the system and\nhigher amounts east with a north-south frontal band that sets up\nsouth of the center of the system, but placement of these features\nis uncertain. A Flood Watch may eventually be needed for portions\nof the area.\n\nSevere Weather/Tropical Tornadoes:\n\nUnsurprisingly, low-level shear and helicity values are high as\nthe tropical system moves in. Mid-level winds/deep-layer shear\nvalues are higher than normal for a tropical system due to\nphasing with the upstream shortwave trough. The main limiting\nfactor as it often is as we move into the cool season is\ninstability. The cold air damming wedge and associated easterly\nwinds resulting in evaporative cooling from isentropic lift\ndriven rainfall will be the primary limiting factor for surface-\nbased instability. At this time, the northeast quadrant of the\nstorm looks stable as it moves in overnight. Starting potentially\nas early as the early morning hours on Saturday in our far\nsoutheast counties, a narrow corridor of instability ma\n--- rainfall amounts char 58628 ---\nmains ongoing over the area, but some areas did receive\nbeneficial rainfall last weekend, especially our far southwest\nand far southeast counties. Streamflows remain below normal to\nwell below normal for this time of year across West Alabama,\nwhile streamflows are closer to normal for this time of year\n(though normal is fairly low this time of year) elsewhere. This\nshould minimize the risk of widespread main stem river flooding.\nStill, with copious tropical moisture transport there will be a\nthreat for flash flooding, especially in urban and other poor\ndrainage areas. Current forecast rainfall amounts range from 2-4\"\nwith locally higher amounts of 5\". There may be a band of\nrelatively lower rainfall amounts due to dry slotting somewhere\nbetween higher amounts west with the core of the system and\nhigher amounts east with a north-south frontal band that sets up\nsouth of the center of the system, but placement of these features\nis uncertain. A Flood Watch may eventually be needed for portions\nof the area.\n\nSevere Weather/Tropical Tornadoes:\n\nUnsurprisingly, low-level shear and helicity values are high as\nthe tropical system moves in. Mid-level winds/deep-layer shear\nvalues are higher than normal for a tropical system due to\nphasing with the upstream shortwave trough. The main limiting\nfactor as it often is as we move into the cool season is\ninstability. The cold air damming wedge 